Build Specification — App 16: Teaching and Training Sandbox
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
An interactive environment (web platform) with the worked examples from both the STAG specification (Artifact 1) and the Null-Model Protocol paper preloaded. It lets a student change the support rule, the null, the scan family, or the partition, and see the effect in real time. It takes in a choice of example and a parameter change, and puts out the recomputed statistic, label, or p-value, with the relevant section shown alongside. It is for courses in methodology, statistics, and philosophy of science.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Preload the worked examples from Artifact 1, Sections 11.1 through 11.5, and from the Null-Model Protocol paper, Sections 11.1 through 11.7.

Present each example as an interactive scenario.

Let the student change parameters and see the effect in real time.

For STAG examples, let the student change the support rule, the null model, the partition, the scan family, the g_min, the boundary convention, the empty-W convention, and the tie-breaking rule.

For Null-Model Protocol examples, let the student change the support rule, the null, the scan family, the g_min, the boundary convention, the empty-W convention, the tail definition, and N.

Recompute the derived display label and the evidential anchoring status for STAG examples.

Recompute the observed statistic, the empirical null distribution, the exceedance probability, the Monte Carlo uncertainty, the empty-W fraction, and the point mass at zero for Null-Model Protocol examples.

Show the relevant section of the specification alongside the recomputed output.

Show the effect of the change as a delta from the original value.

Show a warning when a change makes a label or statistic inconsistent with the specification.

Show the worked-example's original values as a baseline.

Allow the student to reset the parameters to the baseline.

Allow the student to save the current parameter set and the recomputed output.

Allow the student to load a saved parameter set.

Provide a guided tour for each example.

Provide a set of exercises for each example.

Provide the answers to the exercises.

Support multiple students with separate sessions.

Support the instructor mode with the ability to add new examples.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same parameter set produces same output.

Fast: recompute in under one second for STAG examples and under ten seconds for Null-Model Protocol examples with N <= 10^4.

No network dependency for the core functionality.

Versioned: the sandbox records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, and the sandbox version.

Reproducible: the sandbox records the parameter set, the seed, and the output.

Input Specification
Input 1. Example selection. Required. The student selects an example from the preloaded set.

Input 2. Parameter changes. Optional. The student changes one or more parameters.

Input 3. Saved parameter set. Optional. The student loads a previously saved parameter set.

Input 4. Session identifier. Required. A string identifying the student's session.

Input 5. Instructor mode. Optional. Boolean. If true, the student can add new examples.

Input 6. Seed. Required for Null-Model Protocol examples.

Input 7. Output format. Optional. Values: interactive (default), text, json.

Output Specification
Output 1. Interactive display. The example's original values, the current parameter set, the recomputed output, and the delta.

Output 2. Recomputed label. For STAG examples, the derived display label and the evidential anchoring status.

Output 3. Recomputed statistic. For Null-Model Protocol examples, the observed statistic, the empirical null distribution, the exceedance probability, the Monte Carlo uncertainty, the empty-W fraction, and the point mass at zero.

Output 4. Relevant section. The section of the specification that defines the recomputed output.

Output 5. Warnings. Array of warning objects. Each warning has a code, a message, and the offending parameter.

Output 6. Exercises. The exercises for the example.

Output 7. Answers. The answers to the exercises.

Output 8. Saved parameter set. JSON. The parameter set and the recomputed output.

Output 9. Reproducibility record. JSON. The STAG schema version, Null-Model Protocol schema version, sandbox version, parameter set, seed, and output.

